    {
      "persona": "Whitehat Hacker",
      "taskMessage": "<b>Dr. Mira Sen:</b> ICMP tells part of the story. Some hosts reply, some drop echo requests by policy, and some firewall paths rewrite the response before it ever reaches you. A single packet type is not a conclusion.",
      "taskInstructions": "",
      "taskQuestion": "Why is ping discovery not final proof that a host is absent?",
      "taskCompleteText": "<b>Dr. Mira Sen:</b> Correct. A host can block ICMP while still exposing live services.",
      "answers": [
        "ICMP replies are required before any TCP port can open.",
        "Private addresses are unable to respond to a ping sweep.",
        "Nmap skips live hosts unless ICMP is explicitly allowed.",
        "A host can block ICMP but still expose live services."
      ],
      "correctAnswer": 3
    },
    {
      "persona": "Blackhat Hacker",
      "taskMessage": "<b>Kade Mercer:</b> A quiet ping is information, not certainty. A host can ignore echo requests while still listening on another service.",
